hing_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the rule was found\n\nEach grid holds three solid rectangles. They are coloured 1, 2 and 4, and every one of them touches the bottom edge. In the output each rectangle has kept its shape, colour and columns, and has moved straight up. The fact sheet backs this up: each colour's cell count is the same before and after, and the only cell changes are a block turning into background in one place and the same block appearing higher up.\n\nSo the question is how far each rectangle moves. I compared the shift with each rectangle's height, taking the top row from the fact sheet's bounding boxes:\n\n| pair | colour | height | top row: in → out | shift |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 4 | 6 | 9 → 3 | 6 |\n| 1 | 2 | 5 | 10 → 5 | 5 |\n| 1 | 1 | 2 | 13 → 11 | 2 |\n| 2 | 4 | 4 | 11 → 7 | 4 |\n| 2 | 1 | 4 | 11 → 7 | 4 |\n| 2 | 2 | 2 | 13 → 11 | 2 |\n| 3 | 4 | 3 | 12 → 9 | 3 |\n| 3 | 1 | 4 | 11 → 7 | 4 |\n| 3 | 2 | 1 | 14 → 13 | 1 |\n\nIn every row the shift equals the height. The amount does not depend on colour: colour 4 moves 6, 4 or 3 depending on how tall it is. The rule is therefore: **each object moves up by exactly its own height.** It ends up sitting directly on top of the space it used to fill.\n\nNo rectangle crosses another while moving, because each one only travels within its own columns. That means the objects can be moved one at a time, in any order.\n\n## Applying it to the test\n\n- The colour‑2 block is 7 rows tall (rows 8–14), so it moves to rows 1–7.\n- The colour‑4 block is 6 rows tall (rows 9–14), so it moves to rows 3–8.\n- The colour‑1 block is 3 rows tall (rows 12–14), so it moves to rows 9–11.\n\n## Construction stages\n\n1. Start from an empty background canvas.\n2. Place each object, one stage per object, at its original box shifted up by its height.\n3. Emit the final canvas and return it.\n\nThe program has not been run against the train pairs yet. The table above suggests it should reproduce all three outputs.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Clear the canvas to background; every object will be re-placed.\")\n    for o in objects(g, bg=bg):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        h = y1 - y0 + 1\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            ny = y - h\n            if 0 <= ny < g.shape[0]:\n                out[ny, x] = o[\"colour\"]\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Move the colour {o['colour']} object (height {h}) up by {h} rows.\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"All objects lifted by their own heights.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"I compared the shift with each rectangle's height, taking the top row from the fact sheet's bounding boxes:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"| pair | colour | height | top row: in → out | shift |"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In every row the shift equals the height."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The fact sheet backs this up: each colour's cell count is the same before and after, and the only cell changes are a block turning into background in one place and the same block appearing higher up."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"I compared the shift with each rectangle's height, taking the top row from the fact sheet's bounding boxes:"}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":" rule is therefore: **each object moves up by exactly its own height.** It ends up sitting directly on top of the space it used to fill."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"4364293336236d832b42aa7e64b74e3bbcab4da36c2a9409fc5ca63e011b0572","task_sha256":"7f2d36467758645e3d5fff4fd5abf6528f82246215528f1e6dd0eb1aee4ca689","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
